= Open-source intelligence =

'''Open-source intelligence''' (OSINT) is intelligence produced from publicly available or lawfully accessible open information to answer a defined intelligence requirement. It is not simply “information found on the Internet,” nor is it equivalent to collecting links, searching social media, or using open-source software.

A long-standing U.S. statutory formulation defines OSINT as intelligence produced from publicly available information that is collected, exploited, and disseminated in a timely manner to an appropriate audience for the purpose of addressing a specific intelligence requirement.<ref name="uscode">[https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-136/t50/s3038/ U.S. Code, Department of Defense Strategy for Open-source Intelligence note.]</ref>

The key distinction is therefore:

<pre>
publicly available information
→ collection and validation
→ analysis
→ intelligence judgment
→ dissemination for a requirement
</pre>

The source may be open; the intelligence work still requires method, provenance, uncertainty assessment, and lawful handling.

== Publicly available information ==

Publicly available information (PAI) can include material that is:

* published or broadcast;
* available on request;
* publicly accessible online;
* available by subscription or purchase;
* observable at public places or events.

Defense Intelligence Agency guidance distinguishes PAI from the OSINT product created by collecting, assessing, and reporting such information for intelligence requirements.<ref name="dia">[https://www.dia.mil/About/Open-Source-Intelligence/ Defense Intelligence Agency, “Open-Source Intelligence.”]</ref>

A newspaper article is PAI.

A geolocated, temporally validated assessment combining that article with imagery, records, and independent observations to answer an intelligence question can be OSINT.

== Open source does not mean free of cost ==

“Open” in OSINT refers to accessibility, not necessarily zero price.

Commercial databases, satellite imagery, corporate registries, subscription archives, and data services may be accessible to members of the public or commercial customers while requiring payment.

The relevant question is whether the information is lawfully obtainable through open or commercial channels under the governing definition and authority.

== Intelligence requirement ==

Professional OSINT begins with a question.

A requirement should define:

* what is being asked;
* why the answer matters;
* geographic and temporal scope;
* confidence needed;
* decision deadline;
* collection constraints.

Without a requirement, analysts can accumulate large amounts of data without producing useful intelligence.

Requirement-driven collection also limits unnecessary data gathering.

== Collection planning ==

A collection plan identifies potential sources before conclusions are formed.

Source classes can include:

* government publications;
* public corporate records;
* academic literature;
* news media;
* social media;
* photographs and video;
* maps;
* satellite and aerial imagery;
* vessel and aircraft tracking data;
* public procurement records;
* archives;
* commercial databases.

The analyst should ask what evidence would support, weaken, or falsify each hypothesis.

Collecting only confirmatory material creates confirmation bias.

== Source provenance ==

Provenance records where information came from and how it was obtained.

Useful provenance fields include:

* original source;
* author or uploader if known;
* URL or identifier;
* publication or upload time;
* collection time;
* archive location;
* file hash;
* transformation history.

A screenshot without provenance is weaker than a preserved original with metadata and traceable handling.

Provenance supports later verification and correction.

== Source versus information reliability ==

Two questions should remain separate:

# Is the source reliable?
# Is this particular piece of information credible?

A historically reliable organization can publish an incorrect report.

An anonymous source can publish authentic evidence.

Analysts should therefore evaluate the item, not substitute source reputation for verification.

== Independent corroboration ==

Multiple reports are not independent if they copy one original claim.

Suppose five news articles repeat one unverified social-media post.

That is one underlying observation plus four republications, not five independent confirmations.

Analysts should trace claims backward toward primary evidence and identify dependency among sources.

Independent corroboration is strongest when different evidence channels converge without sharing the same origin.

== Geolocation ==

Visual evidence can sometimes be geolocated by matching features such as:

* road geometry;
* building shape;
* terrain;
* skylines;
* signage;
* utility infrastructure;
* shadows;
* satellite imagery.

A location claim should record which features were matched and which alternatives were considered.

One visual similarity is not enough for a high-confidence conclusion.

== Chronolocation ==

Chronolocation attempts to establish when an event occurred.

Evidence can include:

* upload metadata;
* shadows and solar position;
* weather;
* vegetation;
* construction state;
* event schedules;
* known before-and-after imagery;
* contemporaneous posts.

Platform timestamps can be misleading because of time zones, reposting, editing, or archival delay.

A publication time is not automatically the event time.

== Image and video verification ==

Digital media can be altered, mislabeled, reposted, or stripped of metadata.

Verification can examine:

* earliest known upload;
* metadata;
* visual landmarks;
* frame consistency;
* prior appearances;
* compression history;
* weather and lighting;
* corroborating imagery.

A cryptographic hash can prove that a preserved file has not changed since hashing.

It cannot prove that the file depicts what the uploader claims.

== Satellite imagery ==

Satellite imagery can support:

* change detection;
* infrastructure analysis;
* damage assessment;
* movement tracking;
* environmental monitoring.

Analysts must account for:

* acquisition date;
* spatial resolution;
* cloud cover;
* viewing angle;
* sensor type;
* georegistration;
* image processing.

Absence of a visible object can result from sensor or timing limitations.

== Social media ==

Social media provides rapid observation but creates major verification problems.

Posts can be:

* fabricated;
* automated;
* coordinated;
* selectively edited;
* reposted without context;
* deleted;
* geotagged incorrectly.

Follower count and virality are not evidence of truth.

Analysts should preserve the original context and avoid treating engagement metrics as credibility scores.

== Search-engine and platform bias ==

Search results are ranked by algorithms.

What an analyst sees depends on:

* language;
* location;
* personalization;
* indexing;
* platform policy;
* query wording;
* commercial ranking.

Search visibility therefore does not equal real-world prevalence.

Important evidence can also be absent from indexed platforms.

== Verification and falsification ==

Strong OSINT analysis seeks disconfirming evidence.

For hypothesis <math>H</math>, the analyst should ask:

* What evidence would be expected if <math>H</math> were true?
* What evidence would be expected if <math>H</math> were false?
* Which alternative explanation fits the observations?
* Which assumptions remain untested?

The objective is not to build the most persuasive story.

It is to determine which explanation best survives the available evidence.

== Confidence ==

Confidence should reflect the quality and independence of evidence, not rhetorical certainty.

A useful assessment distinguishes:

* observation;
* inference;
* assumption;
* confidence.

For example:

<pre>
Observation: imagery shows three vehicles.
Inference: they are likely military logistics vehicles.
Assumption: the imagery date is correctly established.
Confidence: moderate.
</pre>

This structure prevents inferred claims from being repeated later as raw observations.

== Digital open-source investigations ==

OSINT overlaps with journalism, human-rights investigations, legal fact-finding, and academic research.

The Berkeley Protocol on Digital Open Source Investigations provides professional standards for identifying, collecting, preserving, verifying, and analyzing digital open-source information in international criminal, humanitarian, and human-rights investigations.<ref name="berkeley">[https://humanrights.berkeley.edu/publications/berkeley-protocol-on-digital-open-source-investigations/ Human Rights Center and OHCHR, ''Berkeley Protocol on Digital Open Source Investigations''.]</ref>

The Protocol emphasizes legality, methodology, data minimization, preservation, safety, and ethical handling.

Its evidentiary purpose should not be conflated with every form of military, commercial, journalistic, or academic OSINT.

== Evidence preservation ==

When material may later be disputed or removed, preservation should record:

* original content;
* URL;
* timestamp;
* capture method;
* file hash;
* metadata;
* handling history.

Preservation protects the integrity of the record.

It does not transform weak evidence into strong evidence.

== Privacy ==

Public accessibility does not eliminate privacy interests.

A person may publish information for a limited social context without anticipating permanent aggregation, profiling, or exposure.

OSINT practice should therefore consider:

* necessity;
* proportionality;
* data minimization;
* retention;
* downstream harm;
* legal authority.

The fact that information can be collected does not establish that it should be collected or republished.

== Safety ==

Open-source investigators can expose:

* victims;
* witnesses;
* journalists;
* activists;
* military personnel;
* uninvolved civilians.

Publishing exact locations, identities, or routines can create physical risk.

Investigators should minimize unnecessary exposure and separate analytical need from public disclosure.

== Researcher security ==

OSINT work can expose investigators to:

* phishing;
* malware;
* tracking;
* harassment;
* doxxing;
* disturbing content.

Professional workflows may use isolated environments, controlled identities, secure storage, and role-specific access.

Safety includes psychosocial effects from repeated exposure to graphic material.

== Managed attribution ==

Some professional intelligence work uses managed attribution to control what a source website can infer about the collector.

This can include technical separation of research environments and organizational identity.

Such methods are governed by legal and institutional authorities.

OSINT does not authorize deceptive or unauthorized access merely because the objective is intelligence.

== Automation ==

Automation can assist with:

* translation;
* entity extraction;
* image matching;
* deduplication;
* geospatial search;
* archiving;
* anomaly detection.

Automated output should be treated as a candidate result requiring validation.

Machine-generated labels can reproduce training-data bias or hallucinate relationships.

== Artificial intelligence ==

Large language and multimodal models can summarize large source sets and suggest hypotheses.

Risks include:

* fabricated citations;
* false geolocation;
* merged identities;
* unsupported inference;
* loss of source boundaries.

AI-assisted OSINT should preserve source-level provenance so every material assertion can be traced back to evidence.

== Operational security versus transparency ==

OSINT values traceability, but intelligence organizations may need to protect collection methods, analyst identity, or operational context.

This creates a tension between reproducibility and operational security.

A product can preserve internal provenance without publishing every collection detail publicly.

== OSINT and all-source intelligence ==

OSINT can complement classified or restricted intelligence disciplines.

Public evidence can:

* fill gaps;
* independently confirm classified reporting;
* provide contextual background;
* support dissemination to audiences without clearances.

Open-source intelligence should not be treated as inherently inferior or inherently superior to classified collection.

Source quality and relevance determine value.

== OSINT and open-source software ==

[[Open-source software]] is unrelated to the intelligence meaning of “open source.”

Open-source software concerns software licensing and source-code rights.

OSINT concerns intelligence derived from publicly or commercially accessible information.

OSINT tools can themselves be proprietary or open-source software.

== Epistemic boundary ==

OSINT is a methodology for producing intelligence from accessible information.

It does not make public claims automatically reliable.

The decisive work lies in requirement definition, source evaluation, provenance, corroboration, falsification, uncertainty management, ethical handling, and transparent separation of observation from inference.

